<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hasan ibn Zayd</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/884">884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> · Also known as al-Da'i al-Kabir</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hasan ibn Zayd was a descendant of the Prophet's grandson Hasan, invited in 864 by the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruyan">Ruyan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, angered by Tahirid land seizures, to lead them. With Daylamite support he took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amol</a> and founded a Zaydi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imamate">imamate</a>, the Alavid state of Tabaristan, taking the title al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dai">Da'i</a> ila'l-Haqq. His rule spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylamites">Daylamites</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-zayd.jpg" alt="Hasan ibn Zayd" width="320" height="155" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hasan_ibn_Zayd%27s_Dirham-2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hasan was descended from Hasan ibn Zayd ibn Hasan, a great-grandson of Ali, the son-in-law of Muhammad and fourth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>. In 864 he was living in Ray in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a>, a mountainous region on the southern shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, had remained largely untouched by the Muslim conquests of the 7th century and was ruled by the native Dabuyid dynasty until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a> conquered it in 759/60. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamization">Islamization</a> began only after 840 under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahirid-dynasty">Tahirids</a>, the Abbasids' viceroys for the East. Most of its people became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a>, but pro-Alid Shi'ite missionaries also found followers there.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In the 860s the Tahirid Muhammad ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-ibn-tahir">Abdallah ibn Tahir</a> governed western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, with his brother Sulayman ibn Abdallah ibn Tahir as his deputy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gurgan</a>. Resentment of their fiscal agents led in 864 to a rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruyan">Ruyan</a>, Kalar and Chalus under two sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rustam</a>, who called on Hasan to lead them. He was recognised as emir by part of the population and won the allegiance of the Justanid king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a>, Vahsudan ibn Marzuban.</p><p>His rule was repeatedly broken by invasions. Sulayman drove him out in 865, though he recovered the province within the year; the Abbasid general Muflih forced him into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daylam">Daylam</a> in 869; and in 874 Ya'qub al-Saffar, angered that Hasan sheltered his enemy Abdallah al-Sijzi, defeated the Zaydid forces at Sari. Torrential rain and disease soon forced Ya'qub's army to withdraw. From 867 Hasan usually held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorgan">Gurgan</a>, and at times <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rayy">Rayy</a>, Qazvin (865–868) and Qumis (873–879). In Khurasan he backed Abu Talha Mansur ibn Sharkab against Ahmad ibn Abdallah al-Khujistani and shared his defeat in 878/879.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hasan died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amul</a> on 6 January 884 and was succeeded by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-zayd">Muhammad ibn Zayd</a>; the Zaydids ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> until 928. Historians praised him as a just and equitable ruler, but outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruyan">Ruyan</a> and Kalar enthusiasm for his rule seems to have waned quickly, owing to his enforcement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiism">Shi'ism</a>, his repression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> majority and his reliance on Daylamite soldiers.</p><p>Relations with the local Iranian rulers varied. The Qarinids of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabaristan">Tabaristan</a> supported him, the Bavandids in the east were usually hostile, and when the Justanid Khurshid turned against him Hasan had him replaced by his brother Justan. The Orientalist Frants Buhl wrote that Hasan possessed rare energy and a capacity for stubborn resistance, and was sincerely religious, well educated and a patron of letters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hasan ibn Zayd” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 864 the people of Ruyan and Tabaristan, angered by land seizures under Tahirid governors, invited <b>Hasan ibn Zayd</b>, a descendant of the Prophet's grandson Hasan, to lead them.</blockquote><a href="/state/alavid-dynasty">Alavid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amol">Amol</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.47,39.61&amp;year=874" aria-label="Medina on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Hasan ibn Zayd"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5745 5441h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5745 5441h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.47,39.61&amp;year=874">What was here in 874 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
